INT. SAN BERNARDO ABBEY - WINE CELLAR - NIGHT

Barrel-vaulted stone. Rows of dusty casks. A single oil lamp. Somewhere above, the dull thud of artillery, steady as a clock.

SERGEANT CORNELIUS HOLLOWAY, 35, helmet off, Tommy gun across his knees, sits on a cask. PRIVATE BAPTISTE FONTAINE, 20, French-Canadian, shivering, wraps a bandage round his own forearm. CORPORAL YUKI TAKADA, 25, Nisei interpreter, sits on the stairs with an Italian phrasebook.

BROTHER ANSELMO, 70, Benedictine, stout, wine-stained robe, pours three tin cups from a cask. His hands are steady.

HOLLOWAY
Brother, how long have you been in this cellar?

ANSELMO
Since the bombs came. Eleven days.

TAKADA
(translating)
Undici giorni.

ANSELMO
(in English)
Eleven. Yes. Thank you, my son.

Fontaine takes a cup. Holloway takes a cup. He sniffs and drinks.

FONTAINE
Sergeant, he's good.

HOLLOWAY
The wine?

FONTAINE
The wine. And the Brother.

TAKADA
Sergeant, the church above us is clear. No enemy.

HOLLOWAY
Good.

Silence. The artillery thuds. Dust sifts from the vault.

ANSELMO
You are hungry. I have bread.

HOLLOWAY
We're fine.

ANSELMO
I insist.

He disappears behind a rack of casks. Holloway looks at Takada. Takada shakes his head. Fontaine leans toward Holloway.

FONTAINE
Sergeant, he said "eleven days." But the shelling started on the ninth.

HOLLOWAY
Say that again.

FONTAINE
Day nine. The shelling.

Holloway stands. Slowly. His hand finds the Tommy gun.

HOLLOWAY
Corporal. Ask him who the bread is for.

TAKADA
(calling)
Padre, per chi è il pane?

No answer. Takada stands too. Fontaine raises his rifle. The lamp flickers.

TAKADA (CONT'D)
(whisper)
He's not answering.

Holloway edges past the first cask. Fontaine follows him, boots silent on the flagstones. The second rack. The third. A narrow door, ajar, set in the stone.

HOLLOWAY
Brother Anselmo. Come out.

The door swings open. Anselmo stands in the opening, a loaf of bread in each hand. Behind him, a pale OFFICER in a grey-green greatcoat sits on a pallet, leg splinted, a Luger in his fist, pointed at Holloway.

OBERLEUTNANT KRAUSS, 30, blond, sweat-slick, eyes steady.

KRAUSS
(accented)
Gentlemen. Please do not make the Brother choose.

Fontaine's rifle swings to Krauss. Takada's pistol clears the holster. Holloway's Tommy gun rises. Everyone holds.

ANSELMO
(softly)
No more dying in my cellar.

Nobody moves. The artillery thuds. A barrel creaks. Anselmo lowers the bread to the stone floor, one loaf and then the other, and raises both open hands between the guns.

HOLLOWAY
Brother, step aside.

ANSELMO
No.

KRAUSS
He's a stubborn man.

FONTAINE
Sergeant, I can't see a shot.

HOLLOWAY
Then don't take it.

TAKADA
(to Krauss, in German)
Wie lange hier?

KRAUSS
Eleven days.

Takada closes his eyes for a long moment.

TAKADA
Sergeant. He was telling the truth.

HOLLOWAY
He was telling us something.

He lowers the Tommy gun an inch. Fontaine does not. Krauss does not. The oil lamp gutters.
